    15	<a name="introduction"></a>
    16	## Introduction
    17	
    18	When you start a new Laravel project, error and exception handling is already configured for you; however, at any point, you may use the `withExceptions` method in your application's `bootstrap/app.php` to manage how exceptions are reported and rendered by your application.
    19	
    20	The `$exceptions` object provided to the `withExceptions` closure is an instance of `Illuminate\Foundation\Configuration\Exceptions` and is responsible for managing exception handling in your application. We'll dive deeper into this object throughout this documentation.
    21	
    22	<a name="configuration"></a>
    23	## Configuration
    24	
    25	The `debug` option in your `config/app.php` configuration file determines how much information about an error is actually displayed to the user. By default, this option is set to respect the value of the `APP_DEBUG` environment variable, which is stored in your `.env` file.
    26	
    27	During local development, you should set the `APP_DEBUG` environment variable to `true`.
    28	
    29	> [!WARNING]
    30	> In your production environment, the value of `APP_DEBUG` should always be `false`. If the value is set to `true` in production, you risk exposing sensitive configuration values to your application's end users.
    31	
    32	<a name="handling-exceptions"></a>
    33	## Handling Exceptions
    34	
    35	<a name="reporting-exceptions"></a>
    36	### Reporting Exceptions
    37	
    38	In Laravel, exception reporting is used to log exceptions or send them to an external service like [Laravel Nightwatch](https://nightwatch.laravel.com), [Sentry](https://github.com/getsentry/sentry-laravel), or [Flare](https://flareapp.io). By default, exceptions will be logged based on your [logging](/docs/{{version}}/logging) configuration. However, you are free to log exceptions however you wish.
    39	
    40	If you need to report different types of exceptions in different ways, you may use the `report` exception method in your application's `bootstrap/app.php` to register a closure that should be executed when an exception of a given type needs to be reported. Laravel will determine what type of exception the closure reports by examining the type-hint of the closure:
    41	
    42	```php
    43	use App\Exceptions\InvalidOrderException;
    44	
    45	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
    46	    $exceptions->report(function (InvalidOrderException $e) {
    47	        // ...
    48	    });
    49	})
    50	```
    51	
    52	When you register a custom exception reporting callback using the `report` method, Laravel will still log the exception using the default logging configuration for the application. If you wish to stop the propagation of the exception to the default logging stack, you may use the `stop` method when defining your reporting callback or return `false` from the callback:
    53	
    54	```php
    55	use App\Exceptions\InvalidOrderException;
    56	
    57	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
    58	    $exceptions->report(function (InvalidOrderException $e) {
    59	        // ...
    60	    })->stop();
    61	
    62	    $exceptions->report(function (InvalidOrderException $e) {
    63	        return false;
    64	    });
    65	})
    66	```
    67	
    68	> [!NOTE]
    69	> To customize the exception reporting for a given exception, you may also utilize [reportable exceptions](/docs/{{version}}/errors#renderable-exceptions).
    70	
    71	<a name="global-log-context"></a>
    72	#### Global Log Context
    73	
    74	If available, Laravel automatically adds the current user's ID to every exception's log message as contextual data. You may define your own global contextual data using the `context` exception method in your application's `bootstrap/app.php` file. This information will be included in every exception's log message written by your application:
    75	
    76	```php
    77	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
    78	    $exceptions->context(fn () => [
    79	        'foo' => 'bar',
    80	    ]);
    81	})
    82	```
    83	
    84	<a name="exception-log-context"></a>
    85	#### Exception Log Context
    86	
    87	While adding context to every log message can be useful, sometimes a particular exception may have unique context that you would like to include in your logs. By defining a `context` method on one of your application's exceptions, you may specify any data relevant to that exception that should be added to the exception's log entry:
    88	
    89	```php
    90	<?php
    91	
    92	namespace App\Exceptions;
    93	
    94	use Exception;
    95	
    96	class InvalidOrderException extends Exception
    97	{
    98	    // ...
    99	
   100	    /**
   101	     * Get the exception's context information.
   102	     *
   103	     * @return array<string, mixed>
   104	     */
   105	    public function context(): array
   106	    {
   107	        return ['order_id' => $this->orderId];
   108	    }
   109	}
   110	```
   111	
   112	<a name="the-report-helper"></a>
   113	#### The `report` Helper
   114	
   115	Sometimes you may need to report an exception but continue handling the current request. The `report` helper function allows you to quickly report an exception without rendering an error page to the user:
   116	
   117	```php
   118	public function isValid(string $value): bool
   119	{
   120	    try {
   121	        // Validate the value...
   122	    } catch (Throwable $e) {
   123	        report($e);
   124	
   125	        return false;
   126	    }
   127	}
   128	```
   129	
   130	<a name="deduplicating-reported-exceptions"></a>
   131	#### Deduplicating Reported Exceptions
   132	
   133	If you are using the `report` function throughout your application, you may occasionally report the same exception multiple times, creating duplicate entries in your logs.
   134	
   135	If you would like to ensure that a single instance of an exception is only ever reported once, you may invoke the `dontReportDuplicates` exception method in your application's `bootstrap/app.php` file:
   136	
   137	```php
   138	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
   139	    $exceptions->dontReportDuplicates();
   140	})
   141	```
   142	
   143	Now, when the `report` helper is called with the same instance of an exception, only the first call will be reported:
   144	
   145	```php
   146	$original = new RuntimeException('Whoops!');
   147	
   148	report($original); // reported
   149	
   150	try {
   151	    throw $original;
   152	} catch (Throwable $caught) {
   153	    report($caught); // ignored
   154	}
   155	
   156	report($original); // ignored
   157	report($caught); // ignored
   158	```
   159	
   160	<a name="exception-log-levels"></a>
   161	### Exception Log Levels
   162	
   163	When messages are written to your application's [logs](/docs/{{version}}/logging), the messages are written at a specified [log level](/docs/{{version}}/logging#log-levels), which indicates the severity or importance of the message being logged.
   164	
   165	As noted above, even when you register a custom exception reporting callback using the `report` method, Laravel will still log the exception using the default logging configuration for the application; however, since the log level can sometimes influence the channels on which a message is logged, you may wish to configure the log level that certain exceptions are logged at.
   166	
   167	To accomplish this, you may use the `level` exception method in your application's `bootstrap/app.php` file. This method receives the exception type as its first argument and the log level as its second argument:
   168	
   169	```php
   170	use PDOException;
   171	use Psr\Log\LogLevel;
   172	
   173	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
   174	    $exceptions->level(PDOException::class, LogLevel::CRITICAL);
   175	})
   176	```
   177	
   178	<a name="ignoring-exceptions-by-type"></a>
   179	### Ignoring Exceptions by Type
   180	
   181	When building your application, there will be some types of exceptions you never want to report. To ignore these exceptions, you may use the `dontReport` exception method in your application's `bootstrap/app.php` file. Any class provided to this method will never be reported; however, they may still have custom rendering logic:
   182	
   183	```php
   184	use App\Exceptions\InvalidOrderException;
   185	
   186	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
   187	    $exceptions->dontReport([
   188	        InvalidOrderException::class,
   189	    ]);
   190	})
   191	```
   192	
   193	Alternatively, you may simply "mark" an exception class with the `Illuminate\Contracts\Debug\ShouldntReport` interface. When an exception is marked with this interface, it will never be reported by Laravel's exception handler:
   194	
   195	```php
   196	<?php
   197	
   198	namespace App\Exceptions;
   199	
   200	use Exception;
   201	use Illuminate\Contracts\Debug\ShouldntReport;
   202	
   203	class PodcastProcessingException extends Exception implements ShouldntReport
   204	{
   205	    //
   206	}
   207	```
   208	
   209	If you need even more control over when a particular type of exception is ignored, you may provide a closure to the `dontReportWhen` method:
   210	
   211	```php
   212	use App\Exceptions\InvalidOrderException;
   213	use Throwable;
   214	
   215	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
   216	    $exceptions->dontReportWhen(function (Throwable $e) {
   217	        return $e instanceof PodcastProcessingException &&
   218	               $e->reason() === 'Subscription expired';
   219	    });
   220	})
   221	```
   222	
   223	Internally, Laravel already ignores some types of errors for you, such as exceptions resulting from 404 HTTP errors, 403 HTTP responses generated by origin mismatches, or 419 HTTP responses generated by invalid CSRF tokens. If you would like to instruct Laravel to stop ignoring a given type of exception, you may use the `stopIgnoring` exception method in your application's `bootstrap/app.php` file:
   224	
   225	```php
   226	use Symfony\Component\HttpKernel\Exception\HttpException;
   227	
   228	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
   229	    $exceptions->stopIgnoring(HttpException::class);
   230	})
   231	```
   232	
   233	<a name="rendering-exceptions"></a>
   234	### Rendering Exceptions
   235	
   236	By default, the Laravel exception handler will convert exceptions into an HTTP response for you. However, you are free to register a custom rendering closure for exceptions of a given type. You may accomplish this by using the `render` exception method in your application's `bootstrap/app.php` file.
   237	
   238	The closure passed to the `render` method should return an instance of `Illuminate\Http\Response`, which may be generated via the `response` helper. Laravel will determine what type of exception the closure renders by examining the type-hint of the closure:
   239	
   240	```php
   241	use App\Exceptions\InvalidOrderException;
   242	use Illuminate\Http\Request;
   243	
   244	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
   245	    $exceptions->render(function (InvalidOrderException $e, Request $request) {
   246	        return response()->view('errors.invalid-order', status: 500);
   247	    });
   248	})
   249	```
   250	
   251	You may also use the `render` method to override the rendering behavior for built-in Laravel or Symfony exceptions such as `NotFoundHttpException`. If the closure given to the `render` method does not return a value, Laravel's default exception rendering will be utilized:
   252	
   253	```php
   254	use Illuminate\Http\Request;
   255	use Symfony\Component\HttpKernel\Exception\NotFoundHttpException;
   256	
   257	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
   258	    $exceptions->render(function (NotFoundHttpException $e, Request $request) {
   259	        if ($request->is('api/*')) {
   260	            return response()->json([
   261	                'message' => 'Record not found.'
   262	            ], 404);
   263	        }
   264	    });
   265	})
   266	```
   267	
   268	<a name="rendering-exceptions-as-json"></a>
   269	#### Rendering Exceptions as JSON
   270	
   271	When rendering an exception, Laravel will automatically determine if the exception should be rendered as an HTML or JSON response based on the `Accept` header of the request. If you would like to customize how Laravel determines whether to render HTML or JSON exception responses, you may utilize the `shouldRenderJsonWhen` method:
   272	
   273	```php
   274	use Illuminate\Http\Request;
   275	use Throwable;
   276	
   277	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
   278	    $exceptions->shouldRenderJsonWhen(function (Request $request, Throwable $e) {
   279	        if ($request->is('admin/*')) {
   280	            return true;
   281	        }
   282	
   283	        return $request->expectsJson();
   284	    });
   285	})
   286	```
   287	
   288	<a name="customizing-the-exception-response"></a>
   289	#### Customizing the Exception Response
   290	
   291	Rarely, you may need to customize the entire HTTP response rendered by Laravel's exception handler. To accomplish this, you may register a response customization closure using the `respond` method:
   292	
   293	```php
   294	use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Response;
   295	
   296	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
   297	    $exceptions->respond(function (Response $response) {
   298	        if ($response->getStatusCode() === 419) {
   299	            return back()->with([
   300	                'message' => 'The page expired, please try again.',
   301	            ]);
   302	        }
   303	
   304	        return $response;
   305	    });
   306	})
   307	```
   308	
   309	<a name="renderable-exceptions"></a>
   310	### Reportable and Renderable Exceptions
   311	
   312	Instead of defining custom reporting and rendering behavior in your application's `bootstrap/app.php` file, you may define `report` and `render` methods directly on your application's exceptions. When these methods exist, they will automatically be called by the framework:
   313	
   314	```php
   315	<?php
   316	
   317	namespace App\Exceptions;
   318	
   319	use Exception;
   320	use Illuminate\Http\Request;
   321	use Illuminate\Http\Response;
   322	
   323	class InvalidOrderException extends Exception
   324	{
   325	    /**
   326	     * Report the exception.
   327	     */
   328	    public function report(): void
   329	    {
   330	        // ...
   331	    }
   332	
   333	    /**
   334	     * Render the exception as an HTTP response.
   335	     */
   336	    public function render(Request $request): Response
   337	    {
   338	        return response(/* ... */);
   339	    }
   340	}
   341	```
   342	
   343	If your exception extends an exception that is already renderable, such as a built-in Laravel or Symfony exception, you may return `false` from the exception's `render` method to render the exception's default HTTP response:
   344	
   345	```php
   346	/**
   347	 * Render the exception as an HTTP response.
   348	 */
   349	public function render(Request $request): Response|bool
   350	{
   351	    if (/** Determine if the exception needs custom rendering */) {
   352	
   353	        return response(/* ... */);
   354	    }
   355	
   356	    return false;
   357	}
   358	```
   359	
   360	If your exception contains custom reporting logic that is only necessary when certain conditions are met, you may need to instruct Laravel to sometimes report the exception using the default exception handling configuration. To accomplish this, you may return `false` from the exception's `report` method:
   361	
   362	```php
   363	/**
   364	 * Report the exception.
   365	 */
   366	public function report(): bool
   367	{
   368	    if (/** Determine if the exception needs custom reporting */) {
   369	
   370	        // ...
   371	
   372	        return true;
   373	    }
   374	
   375	    return false;
   376	}
   377	```
   378	
   379	> [!NOTE]
   380	> You may type-hint any required dependencies of the `report` method and they will automatically be injected into the method by Laravel's [service container](/docs/{{version}}/container).
   381	
   382	<a name="throttling-reported-exceptions"></a>
   383	### Throttling Reported Exceptions
   384	
   385	If your application reports a very large number of exceptions, you may want to throttle how many exceptions are actually logged or sent to your application's external error tracking service.
   386	
   387	To take a random sample rate of exceptions, you may use the `throttle` exception method in your application's `bootstrap/app.php` file. The `throttle` method receives a closure that should return a `Lottery` instance:
   388	
   389	```php
   390	use Illuminate\Support\Lottery;
   391	use Throwable;
   392	
   393	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
   394	    $exceptions->throttle(function (Throwable $e) {
   395	        return Lottery::odds(1, 1000);
   396	    });
   397	})
   398	```
   399	
   400	It is also possible to conditionally sample based on the exception type. If you would like to only sample instances of a specific exception class, you may return a `Lottery` instance only for that class:
   401	
   402	```php
   403	use App\Exceptions\ApiMonitoringException;
   404	use Illuminate\Support\Lottery;
   405	use Throwable;
   406	
   407	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
   408	    $exceptions->throttle(function (Throwable $e) {
   409	        if ($e instanceof ApiMonitoringException) {
   410	            return Lottery::odds(1, 1000);
   411	        }
   412	    });
   413	})
   414	```
   415	
   416	You may also rate limit exceptions logged or sent to an external error tracking service by returning a `Limit` instance instead of a `Lottery`. This is useful if you want to protect against sudden bursts of exceptions flooding your logs, for example, when a third-party service used by your application is down:
   417	
   418	```php
   419	use Illuminate\Broadcasting\BroadcastException;
   420	use Illuminate\Cache\RateLimiting\Limit;
   421	use Throwable;
   422	
   423	->withExceptions(function (Exceptions $exceptions): void {
   424	    $exceptions->throttle(function (Throwable $e) {
   425	        if ($e instanceof BroadcastException) {
   426	            return Limit::perMinute(300);
   427	        }
   428	    });
   429	})
   430	```
   431	
   432	By default, limits will use the exception's class as the rate limit key. You can customize this by specifying your own key using the `by` method on the `Limit`:
   433	
   434	```php
